> > I repeat my advice: selectively remove portions of your customizations
> 
> You are probably right, but removing portions of my customizations is not an easy task. I have accumulated a lot of stuff over the years, I am not well versed in Lisp, and reproducing the problem requires manual interaction. If I cannot find a shortcut, I'd rather switch back to Emacs 30 for a while.

The usual way is to delete half of your .emacs and see if that makes
the problem go away; if not, remove the other half and try again.  Now
you know which half contains the culprit.  Now divide that half in 2
halves and delete one of them.  Etc. etc. -- keep going until you have
a small enough portion that causes the problem when it's present and
deleting it makes the problem go away.

It might take time, but any other method will take longer.

> > . how close to the bottom of the window this happens? the last
> >   visible line? the one before that? line number N from the bottom
> >   (and what is N?)?
> 
> I have been testing more. Say the text buffer is 36 lines high and you haven't scrolled yet, so the first visible line under the tab-line is line number 1.
> 
> If you enter a character in lines 1 to 19, the vertical scroll position does not change. Note that 36 / 2 = 18.
> 
>  From lines 20 to 36, it is like you run 'recenter-top-bottom' after inserting the first character.
> 
> It only happens once when you insert the first character. Afterwards there are no scroll jumps anymore.

That must be some optional feature you turn on.  Maybe someone here
will have the bells ring for them, given the description.  Failing
that, I still suggest to use halving to find the culprit.

